    "title": "Charles Barkley weight loss, explained: Inside basketball legend's stunning body transformation",
    "text": "Charles Barkley denounces Minnesota ICE shootings: 'Somebody's gotta step up'Charles Barkley weight loss, explained: Inside basketball legend's stunning body transformation originally appeared on The Sporting News. Add The Sporting News as a Preferred Source by clicking here.AdvertisementCharles Barkley is an NBA legend, but for college basketball fans he has a special role in their hearts.Barkley is the unfiltered, boisterous voice of the NCAA Tournament. Whether he is playfully mispronouncing mid-major school names, struggling to navigate a touchscreen bracket, or offering surprisingly poignant insights into the pressure on these young athletes, he provides the perfect comedic counterpoint to the high-stakes intensity of the Big Dance. For three weeks every spring, Barkley is at the center of the college basketball world.But fans might notice that he looks a little different this time around. Known for his big-time frame, the NBA Hall of Famer has lost a significant amount of weight over the past few years.Here's how Barkley achieved his transformation and where his health stands today.AdvertisementSN's MARCH MADNESS HQ:Live NCAA bracket | TV schedule | Printable PDFBarkley’s weight loss has been one of the most visible physical transformations in sports media over the last few years. At his heaviest post-retirement, Barkley weighed approximately 352 pounds. As of the 2026 NCAA Tournament, he has lost more than 100 pounds, bringing him back toward his NBA playing weight.Barkley has been very candid about his journey, attributing his success to a combination of medical assistance and a lifestyle wake-up call.Barkley began using Mounjaro (tirzepatide), a GLP-1 and GIP receptor agonist. He publicly credited the medication for helping him \"reset\" his relationship with food, noting that it significantly curbed his appetite and allowed him to manage his portions for the first time in decades.AdvertisementBarkley often jokes that his doctor gave him a blunt reality check, previously saying, \"I’ve seen a lot of fat young people, but I don’t see many fat old people.\" This served as the primary motivation to get healthy for his daughter and grandchildren.While the medication was the catalyst, Barkley incorporated more consistent movement — primarily through golf and light gym work — to ensure he maintained muscle mass while dropping fat.SN AWARDS: All-America team | Player of the Year | Coach of the YearHow much weight did Charles Barkley lose?Barkley has lost a total of 85 pounds over the last few years, a transformation that has taken him from a peak post-retirement weight of 352 pounds down to approximately 267 pounds as of early 2026.AdvertisementWhile he initially made headlines in 2023 for losing the first 62 pounds in just six months, he has continued to work toward his goal of reaching 250 pounds — his official NBA playing weight.SN EXPERT BRACKETS:DeCourcy (Arizona) | Bender (Michigan) | Iyer (Arizona) | Gay (UCLA women)Charles Barkley before and afterIn the early 2020s, Barkley was near the highest weight he's ever been.Fast forward to 2026, and Barkley looks in much better shape and is actively endorsing his GLP-1s.MORE: Ranking 10 best NBA Draft prospects in 2026 March MadnessWhat is GLP-1?GLP-1 stands for glucagon-like peptide-1, a hormone naturally produced in your small intestine. These medications — including well-known brands like Ozempic, Wegovy, and Zepbound — work by mimicking this hormone to help regulate the body's metabolic \"thermostat.\"AdvertisementAccording to researchers at Harvard Health, GLP-1 medications function by targeting three primary systems: the pancreas, the brain, and the digestive tract. When you eat, these drugs trigger the pancreas to release insulin, which acts as a vital key to move glucose out of the bloodstream and into your cells for energy. Simultaneously, they interact with the brain's reward centers to mimic natural hormones that signal fullness. This process helps quiet \"food noise\" — the persistent and often intrusive thoughts about eating — making it easier to manage appetite without relying solely on willpower.The medications also physically slow down the rate at which food leaves your stomach, a process known as gastric emptying. By keeping food in the digestive system for a longer period, they create a sustained sensation of physical fullness. This multi-layered approach allows individuals to naturally reduce their calorie intake while avoiding the constant feeling of deprivation that typically accompanies traditional dieting.MORE: The ultimate guide to March Madness",
